────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

• I changed the IR call-lowering path in builder.go and call_inline.go so block-merged calls rebuild CFG
  edges from rewritten terminators, and merged callees now get an explicit continuation block instead of
  returning straight out of the parent process.

  That fixes one real control-flow bug: adpcm_main no longer causes the top-level process to exit
  immediately after an inlined callee return. I verified the compiler still builds with env GOCACHE=/tmp/go-
  build go test ./cmd/mygo ./internal/ir/....

  The ADPCM case is still not fully fixed. env GOCACHE=/tmp/go-build go run ./cmd/mygo sim --sim-max-cycles
  40000 tests/CHStone/adpcm/main.go still produces no hardware stdout, and even at --sim-max-cycles 1000000
  it does not reach the final print. The remaining failure is deeper in the same inlining path for nested
  helper calls inside the ADPCM loop body, so software and hardware still do not match yet for tests/
  CHStone/adpcm/main.go.


› you can check the output of verilog to debug
  stuck hardware states back to specific source-level loops or inlined calls, then fix the lowering bug at
  that boundary instead of guessing.

